## :page_facing_up: Context
**Fix these bugs**
- [x] Type inference issue when all event parameters are disabled.
- - During event tracking, a user has the option to pass parameters associated with the tracked event. Through the `EventParameterName` annotation, `Collar` provides flexibility to pass a custom parameter name and to also disable a parameter - **by default, all parameters are enabled**. When a parameter is disabled, **it should be excluded from the generated code for tracking the event**. In this example, `dayTime` parameter is excluded from tracking.

```
// Example event with `dayTime` parameter disabled

data class Event2(
    @EventParameterName("my_user_id")
    val uuid: String,

    @EventParameterName(enabled = false)
    val dayTime: Int
) : AnalyticsEvent()

// Generated tracking code snippet - only `my_user_id` is tracked, `dayTime` is excluded.

is AnalyticsEvent.Event2 -> Collar.trackEvent(
      eventName = "event2",
      params = mapOf(
        "my_user_id" to event.uuid
      ),
     transientData =  mapOf<String,Nothing>()
    )
```
The issue was, if all parameters are disabled like in this example, then we end up with an empty map i.e `mapOf()` that doesn't specify a type. With the fix, the type is added so that we end up with `mapOf<String,Nothing>()`

```
// Example event with all parameters disabled

data class Event2(
    @EventParameterName("my_user_id", enabled = false)
    val uuid: String,

    @EventParameterName(enabled = false)
    val dayTime: Int
) : AnalyticsEvent()

// Generated tracking code snippet - the second argument `mapOf()` has no type specified. 

is AnalyticsEvent.Event2 -> Collar.trackEvent(
      eventName = "event2",
      params = mapOf(),
      transientData = mapOf<String,Nothing>()
    )
```

- [x] Empty key for event parameter name when the default `EventParameterName` annotation is used or the user sets `""` for the name.
- - As mentioned above, `Collar` allows the use of custom parameter names. In the above example, `my_user_id` will be used as the custom name for `uuid` parameter. The issue was, if a user passed an empty string for the parameter name as shown below, then the empty string will be used as the parameter name. Same case for when the default value (**which is an empty string**) of the `EventParameterName` annotation is used. With the fix, **the property name in `snake_case` will be used** i.e `day_time` which is the default case when a custom name isn't provided.
```
// Example event with empty string for `dayTime` parameter custom name

data class Event2(
    @EventParameterName("my_user_id")
    val uuid: String,

    @EventParameterName("")
    val dayTime: Int
) : AnalyticsEvent()

// Generated tracking code snippet - empty string is used as the custom parameter name for `dayTime`

is AnalyticsEvent.Event2 -> Collar.trackEvent(
      eventName = "event2",
      params = mapOf(
        "my_user_id" to event.uuid,
        "" to event.dayTime
      ),
      transientData = mapOf<String,Nothing>()
    )
```

## :pencil: Changes

1. Moved the `enabled parameter` check to the `collector` as opposed to the `spec`. This way, the spec will properly call `eventParametersEmpty` instead of first adding an empty map i.e `mapOf()` in anticipation of adding parameters which would cause the issue when nothing is added to the map.
2. Changed the `null` check for event parameter name to an `empty` check

## :no_entry_sign: Breaking
This is not a breaking change.

The TypeScript GitHub Actions job was failing because multiple overlay/grid style definitions referenced `StyleSheet.absoluteFillObject`, which is not present in the current React Native type surface used by CI. This change aligns those components with the supported absolute-fill API without changing layout behavior.

- **TypeScript compatibility**
  - Replaced `StyleSheet.absoluteFillObject` spreads with `StyleSheet.absoluteFill` in the affected overlay and puzzle grid components.
  - Covers shared app overlays plus the play grids for Minesweeper, Nonogram, Sudoku, Takuzu, and Word Search.

- **Scope**
  - Limits the change to style declarations only.
  - Preserves the existing absolute-position/inset behavior for borders, fills, backdrops, and overlays.

- **Example**
  ```ts
  const styles = StyleSheet.create({
    backdrop: {
      ...StyleSheet.absoluteFill,
      backgroundColor: withAlpha(theme.background, 0.72),
    },
  });
  ```

## Why

Three things readers could see were wrong, and all three trace back to how the app reads EPUB files:

- **The reader's font picker offered Lora, Literata and Charter, and every one of them rendered in Roboto.** Quire passed Readium a font name but never the font file, so the page fell back to the WebView's default.
- **Project Gutenberg books turned red after the first tap.** Every chapter of every book went through the WebView's forgiving HTML parser, which reads Gutenberg's empty chapter anchor `<a id="chap01"/>` as a link wrapping the whole chapter. The book's `a:hover { color: red }` then fires on the first tap.
- **Books added from a file showed their file name as the title, with no author.** The parser that reads a book's own metadata threw on Android before it began, a failure the JVM tests could not see. The same failure stripped the ISBN, language and series from every AI insight lookup.

## What changes

**Reader fonts actually render.** Lora, Literata and Charis SIL ship with the reader and are declared to Readium under the names the picker uses, each with real italic and bold. "Charter" becomes "Charis": no trusted source publishes Charter itself, and Charis SIL is SIL's free extension of it. A saved Charter choice loads as Charis. All eight files are unmodified OFL 1.1 fonts from google/fonts, with license texts beside them and credits in NOTICE. The APK grows by about 2.7 MB.

**The app's own screens use the same full Lora.** The library title, cover placeholders and headers drew from a trimmed 280-glyph copy that dropped most accented and all Cyrillic letters. That copy also kept the reserved name "Lora", which Lora's license does not allow for a modified font. They now load the bundled variable font, with SemiBold drawn at the font's real 600 weight. The APK sheds 59 KB of that copy.

**Well-formed chapters use the strict XHTML parser again.**
- On a book's first open, each chapter is checked. Only chapters the strict parser would reject go to the HTML parser; everything else is read as real XHTML, where `<a/>` closes.
- The check covers everything Android's XML parser lets through but Chromium's rejects, each case confirmed on the emulator:
  - a second root element, a truncated document, repeated attributes;
  - forbidden characters, raw or as a reference;
  - named entities with no DTD to resolve them;
  - chapters declaring a legacy encoding, such as Shift_JIS, that Readium's UTF-8 round trip breaks.
- When in doubt, a chapter goes to HTML, which is the old behaviour.
- The result is cached per book file, so the check runs on a book's first open only. That costs about half a second for Shakespeare's complete works (279 chapters) and 20 to 70 ms for a typical book; later opens cost what they did before.

**Book metadata is read on Android.** The parser setup call that throws on Android is now guarded, as it already was in the identity extractor and the sideload importer. A new test swaps in a parser factory that throws the way Android's does.

## What readers will notice

- Font choices take effect, and the app's own text draws accented and Cyrillic letters in Lora.
- Gutenberg books stay black when tapped.
- Books added from a file show their real title and author.
- New AI insight cards get the book's ISBN, language and series; cards already generated stay as they are.

Merging cuts a release to GitHub and F-Droid.

## Testing

- `test lint :app:assembleDebug` on the combined branch: 1,271 unit tests with 0 failures; lint reports no errors.
- Unit tests cover:
  - every picker entry asks for a declared family, and every font file ships;
  - the Charter-to-Charis migration;
  - the UI font's weight settings;
  - the XHTML check against Gutenberg-style, malformed and encoding fixtures;
  - the verdict cache;
  - the metadata extractor under an Android-like parser.
- On the emulator:
  - which font file drew each glyph, read over WebView devtools, for System, Lora, Literata, Charis and OpenDyslexic;
  - Alice's chapters before (fully red after one tap) and after (no red pixels);
  - a test book with malformed and well-formed chapters, and one per encoding case;
  - "Łódź Příliš Tiếng Việt Война" in the app's own Lora;
  - the combined build:
    - Alice, served as strict XHTML, draws from the bundled Lora and Charis files, with italics from their italic files, and stays black after taps;
    - the malformed chapter falls back to HTML with the picked font;
    - a book imported from a file shows "Alice's Adventures in Wonderland" by Lewis Carroll, where the build without the metadata fix showed "pg11-images-3.epub".

## Not in this PR

- A malformed chapter that also contains `<a id="x"/>` stays on the HTML parser, so it can still turn red. Expanding self-closing tags in relaxed chapters only would close that.
- Chapters in UTF-16, or in legacy single-byte encodings, render garbled or not at all, under either parser. Readium's UTF-8 round trip causes this, so it needs a fix upstream.

Hello and thank you for the lib! This is to solve the following issue:

1. As soon as we start using `@SerialName`, like downbelow for example:
```kotlin
@JsonApiX(type = "address")
@Serializable
data class Address(
    @SerialName("street") val streetName: String,
    val number: Int,
    val country: String,
    val city: String
) : JsonApiModel()
```
2. Our code gen looks like this (please notice annotation doubling) :
```kotlin
@Serializable
@SerialName(value = "Attributes_address")
public data class Attributes_Address(
  @SerialName(value = "city")
  public val city: String,
  @SerialName(value = "country")
  public val country: String,
  @SerialName(value = "number")
  public val number: Int,
  @property:SerialName(value = "street")
  @SerialName(value = "streetName")
  public val streetName: String
) : Attributes {
  public companion object {
    public fun fromOriginalObject(originalObject: Address) = Attributes_Address(city =
        originalObject.city, country = originalObject.country, number = originalObject.number,
        streetName = originalObject.streetName)
  }
}
```

With the fix, the code generated will look like this:

```kotlin
@Serializable
@SerialName(value = "Attributes_address")
public data class Attributes_Address(
  @SerialName(value = "city")
  public val city: String,
  @SerialName(value = "country")
  public val country: String,
  @SerialName(value = "number")
  public val number: Int,
  @property:SerialName(value = "street")
  public val streetName: String
) : Attributes {
  public companion object {
    public fun fromOriginalObject(originalObject: Address) = Attributes_Address(city =
        originalObject.city, country = originalObject.country, number = originalObject.number,
        streetName = originalObject.streetName)
  }
}
```

I understand it's not perfect solution, but it works and I'm lazy :)

I tweaked out that some functionality like log info and warning doesn't work with the current version.

since 9c3e31db484cad5336ee984a877a62150bb1761c info, warnings and other logs without an error (null argument) will result in an internal exception in following line:

```
 private void process(ThrowableWrapper error, Map<String, Object> custom, String description,
      Level level, boolean isUncaught) {
    ...
    // Pre filter
    if (config.filter() != null && config.filter().preProcess(level, error.getThrowable(), custom,
        description)) {
      LOGGER.debug("Pre-filtered error: {}", error);
      return;
    }
    ...
}
```

Due error could be null the wrapper getThrowable() call will throw a null pointer exception.

**actual:**
rollbar.info("test") -> doesn't appears in rollbar dashboard

**expected:**
rollbar.info("test") -> does appear in rollbar dashboard


### Additional:
I also added nullable annotation to filter preProcess function due better kotlin usage. 

actual:
```
preProcess(
            level: Level,
            error: Throwable,
            custom: MutableMap<String, Any>,
            description: String
          )
```
This results in an illegal argument exception if java calls the interface with a null argument. 
E.g. preProcess(.., null, null, null) -> Exception

now with nullable annotation we have to implement like this:
```
object : Filter {
          override fun postProcess(data: Data?): Boolean {
            return false
          }

          override fun preProcess(
            level: Level,
            error: Throwable?,
            custom: MutableMap<String, Any>?,
            description: String?
          ): Boolean {
            return (error is UnknownHostException)
          }
}
```

Some libs (_e.g._, okhttp) have methods with names that generate invalid typescript code, this PR fixes this by wrapping invalid fields and methods names in quotes

Example:
```diff
-		public -deprecated_proxy(): java.net.Proxy;
+		public "-deprecated_proxy"(): java.net.Proxy;
```

