# PR Request to fix issue #68 

## What's it do?

As the heading states, this PR will fix the issue brought up by vinayakkgarg, where they discovered the **Settings** button in the toolbar was not doing anything when clicked.

## What was wrong?

After looking into the code, I realized when a user clicks the button, the method it hits returns true without actually doing anything:
```java
@Override 
public boolean onOptionsItemSelected(MenuItem item) { 
    // Handle action bar item clicks here. The action bar will 
    // automatically handle clicks on the Home/Up button, so long 
    // as you specify a parent activity in AndroidManifest.xml. 
    int id = item.getItemId(); 
 
    //noinspection SimplifiableIfStatement 
    if (id == R.id.action_settings) { 
        return true; 
    } 
 
    return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item); 
}
```

## How's it fix it?

1. I tried removing the menu and the code associated (such as the method shown above) but then the toolbar was looking wonky and out of shape.

2. To fix the toolbar I downloaded an svg of the DigitalOcean logo and after cleaning it up a bit added it to the project for the new _@drawable/ic_digitalocean_. 

3. Lastly, I had to adjust the measurements for the toolbar in order to have margins on smaller screens so it's not too cramped.

This issue was identified during the implementation of the `undo()` feature in #7746.

Currently, `TextField#cut()`, `TextField#paste()`, and `TextField#delete()` update the cursor and selection positions before ensuring the text change is actually accepted. If a `ChangeListener` cancels the event, the text reverts to its original value, but the cursor remains at the "drifted" position.

This PR ensures that internal state updates (cursor, selection) only occur if `changeText()` returns true.

Changes are below:
`delete(boolean)`: Modified to return the current cursor position if the change is cancelled, and skip `clearSelection()`.
`cut()`: Inherits the fix from the updated `delete()` logic.
`paste(String, boolean)`: Added a check for the `changeText()` return value before updating the cursor position.

This PR also fixed a logical bug in:
https://github.com/libgdx/libgdx/blob/9f5ddf1dae56cabc9c6c8feb62eea7bed7f0c91c/gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/scenes/scene2d/ui/TextField.java#L1210-L1216

where the `else if` branch will never be reached. (because `changeText()` returns false and `text` reverts to `oldText`, thus `.equals()` always returns **true**) 

I have verified this fix basically using a custom GdxTest. When a listener calls `event.cancel()`, the text, cursor, and selection now remain perfectly synchronized at their original positions.

Here's the test code:
<details>
<summary>TextAreaTest4.java</summary>

``` java
// Imports omitted...

public class TextAreaTest4 extends GdxTest {
    private Stage stage;
    private Skin skin;
    private boolean cancelled = false;

    @Override
    public void create () {
        stage = new Stage();
        Gdx.input.setInputProcessor(stage);
        skin = new Skin(Gdx.files.internal("data/uiskin.json"));

        final TextArea textArea = new TextArea("Line 1\nLine 2\n0123456789\nABCDEFGHIJ", skin);
        textArea.setBounds(20, 20, 300, 200);

        textArea.addListener(new ChangeListener() {
            @Override
            public void changed(ChangeEvent event, Actor actor) {
                if (cancelled) {
                    event.cancel();
                    Gdx.app.log("Test", "ChangeEvent Cancelled.");
                }
            }
        });

        Table table = new Table();
        table.setFillParent(true);
        table.top().right().pad(20);

        final CheckBox cancelToggle = new CheckBox(" Cancel Event", skin);
        cancelToggle.addListener(new ChangeListener() {
            @Override
            public void changed(ChangeEvent event, Actor actor) {
                cancelled = cancelToggle.isChecked();
            }
        });

        final Label statusLabel = new Label("", skin);

        table.add(cancelToggle).left().row();
        table.add(new Label("--- Status ---", skin)).padTop(10).row();
        table.add(statusLabel).left();

        stage.addActor(textArea);
        stage.addActor(table);

        stage.getRoot().addCaptureListener(new InputListener() {
            @Override
            public boolean mouseMoved(InputEvent event, float x, float y) {
                updateStatus(textArea, statusLabel);
                return false;
            }
            @Override
            public boolean keyTyped(InputEvent event, char character) {
                updateStatus(textArea, statusLabel);
                return false;
            }
        });
    }

    private void updateStatus(TextArea textArea, Label label) {
        String info = "Cursor: " + textArea.getCursorPosition() + "\n"
                + "Selection: " + textArea.getSelectionStart() + "\n"
                + "Text Len: " + textArea.getText().length();
        label.setText(info);
    }

    @Override
    public void render () {
        ScreenUtils.clear(0.2f, 0.2f, 0.2f, 1);
        stage.act(Gdx.graphics.getDeltaTime());
        stage.draw();
    }

    @Override
    public void resize (int width, int height) {
        stage.getViewport().update(width, height, true);
    }

    @Override
    public void dispose () {
        stage.dispose();
        skin.dispose();
    }
}
```
</details>

Note: The `undo()` method is not included in this PR because the fix was already integrated into #7746.

**What does this PR address? (Why)**
Local.getDefsForLocalUse(graph, stmt) determines the reaching definition statements for a local variable used at a given statement stmt. The BFS traversal queue was previously initialized with stmt itself (queue.add(stmt)). If stmt is an assignment that also defines the same local variable (e.g. x = x + 1), the search encountered stmt.getDef() on its very first pop, added stmt to the reaching definitions, and executed continue, skipping traversal of stmt predecessors.

This creates cyclical, self-referential def-use relationships where an expression claims to be defined by its own assignment before that assignment has taken place. This breaks constant propagation, dead code elimination, and alias analysis passes that depend on accurate reaching definitions, causing them to treat variables as uninitialized or incorrectly initialized.

**What are the main implementation details? (What, How)**
1. Initialized the BFS queue with graph.predecessors(stmt) instead of stmt.
2. This searches all inbound control flow edges reaching the statement without falsely treating the statement own definition as reaching its own operands.
3. If the statement is part of a loop (e.g. back-edge from a later iteration), the statement definition can still be reached via traversal of the predecessor back-edge.


**Steps to Reproduce**

1. Source for Subject File
```java

public class SelfDefinition {
    public static int increment() {
        int x = 5;
        x = x + 1;
        return x;
    }
}
```

2. SootUp Test Code to Reproduce
```java
    JavaView view = new JavaView(inputLocation);
    JavaIdentifierFactory idFactory = view.getIdentifierFactory();
    JavaClassType classType = idFactory.getClassType("example.SelfDefinition");
    JavaSootClass sootClass = view.getClass(classType).orElseThrow();
    JavaSootMethod method = sootClass.getMethod("increment", Collections.emptyList()).orElseThrow();

    Body body = method.getBody();
    Body.BodyBuilder builder = Body.builder(body, Collections.emptySet());
    ControlFlowGraph<?> cfg = builder.getControlFlowGraph();
    Stmt s0 = body.getStmts().get(0);
    Stmt s1 = body.getStmts().get(1);
    Local x = (Local) s0.getDef().get();
    System.out.println("Testing getDefsForLocalUse on stmt: " + s1);
    System.out.println("Defs for local x: " + x.getDefsForLocalUse(cfg, s1));
    // before the fix: Defs for local x: [x = x + 1]
    // after the fix: Defs for local x: [x = 5]
```

