depends on https://github.com/soot-oss/SootUp/pull/1751 , in draft - please review once #1751 is reviewed

**What does this PR address? (Why)**
Methods containing long chains of sequential conditional diamond branches (such as Kotlin data class copy() methods
with 30+ default arguments or state machines) cause LocalLivenessAnalyser.java to hang indefinitely or crash with
OutOfMemoryError during backwards liveness analysis, halting compilation and downstream optimization passes.

root cause(Gemini assisted): LocalLivenessAnalyser.java used a naive FIFO queue that only verified !visitedStmts.contains(pred)
before enqueuing predecessors, but statements were only marked in visitedStmts upon being popped,
with no tracking of nodes currently pending in the queue (inWorklist). When both branches of a conditional diamond
have equal path lengths between the merge target and the branch statement (e.g. branches with balanced statement counts),
backwards traversal reaches the branch statement along both paths at the exact same depth before it has been popped.
As a result, the branch statement is enqueued twice. Across N sequential diamonds, this duplication compounds exponentially (O(2^N)),
growing to over 10 billion operations for 30 conditions, rather than converging in linear time.

**What are the main implementation details? (What, How)**
1. Added an explicit membership tracking set (inWorklist) ensuring no statement is enqueued more than once concurrently.
2. Only re-enqueue predecessors if the statement live-in set has actually changed (!in.equals(liveIn.get(stmt))).
3. Seeded the initial worklist with all CFG nodes to guarantee analysis of disconnected components and exitless infinite loops.

**Steps to Reproduce**
1. Subject File
A Java/Kotlin class with a method containing multiple sequential conditional merge points:
```java
package example;
public class SequentialDiamonds {
    public static int compute(int c) {
        int r = 0;
